# Lesson 4, new exercise: the lesson's "nothing to lose" layout (K5 opposite 863). East leads a low spade; play dummy's king.
title: Nothing to lose: play the king
id: L04-02
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.3
student: N
board: 56
dealer: W
vulnerable: none

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  863    AK72   A3     KQJ5
  E  AJ942  T98    72     A94
  S  K5     Q54    KQJ84  863
  W  QT7    J63    T965   T72

auction:
  W Pass
  N 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 17 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  E Pass
  S 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so East will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 11-12
  spades: 0-1 = Dummy's king, if East, who led the suit, has the ace.
  hearts: 3 = The ace, king and queen.
  diamonds: 5 = Dummy's long suit. Cash your ace first, the high card from the short side.
  clubs: 3 = Three, once the ace is gone. But giving up the lead could be dangerous here.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: East led a low spade. Who might have the ♠A?
play:
  1   E S4  S SK*  W S7  N S3
      SK key: Nothing to lose. If East has the ace, the king wins now. If West has it, the king was never going to win a trick.
      SK not *: Did you think about playing the king? If you play low, West plays a high spade, and later the ace captures your king. If East has the ace, the king wins right now.
  2   S D4  W D5  N DA  E D2
      note: With the ♠K in, you have nine tricks without ever giving up the lead: one spade, three hearts and five diamonds. Leave the clubs alone. Start the diamonds with your ace, the high card from the short side.
  3   N D3  E D7  S DK  W D6
      note: Back to dummy's diamonds.
  4   S DQ  W D9  N C5  E S2
      note: Keep cashing diamonds. Throw a club from your hand: you won't need clubs.
  5   S DJ  W DT  N S6  E C4
      note: The fourth diamond.
  6   S D8  W C2  N S8  E C9
      note: And the fifth.
  7   S HQ  W H3  N H2  E H8
      note: Now the hearts. Dummy's queen first, the high card from the short side.
  8   S H4  W H6  N HA  E H9
      note: Over to your ace and king.
  9   N HK  E HT  S H5  W HJ
      note: Both opponents followed three times, so your last heart is a winner too.
  10  N H7  E S9  S C3  W C7
      note: That's ten tricks. The rest belong to the defense.
  11  N CK  E CA  S C6  W CT
      note: Nothing left to win. Give up the rest.
  12  E SA  S S5  W ST  N CQ
  13  E SJ  S C8  W SQ  N CJ
      note: 10 tricks: 3NT made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: play dummy's ♠K at once, then take your nine tricks without
  giving up the lead.

  The king was the key. With the ace in front of it, East's hand, the king
  wins. If West had held the ace, the king would have been lost whatever you
  did, so there was nothing to lose. Had you played low, West would have won
  with the ♠Q and led another spade, and East's ace would have captured your
  king. Then you would have had to knock out the ♣A, and East would have
  cashed the rest of the spades.
